### What is a daemon thread?

A daemon thread is a background service thread that the JVM does not wait for. When all non-daemon threads have terminated, the JVM exits, killing all daemon threads abruptly. Daemon threads are used for housekeeping tasks that should stop automatically when the application shuts down.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class DaemonThreadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread cacheRefresher = new Thread(() -> {
            while (true) {
                try {
                    System.out.println("Refreshing exchange rate cache...");
                    Thread.sleep(1000);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    break;
                }
            }
        }, "cache-refresher");

        cacheRefresher.setDaemon(true); // MUST be before start()
        cacheRefresher.start();

        Thread.sleep(2500); // main thread sleeps 2.5 seconds
        System.out.println("Main thread done. JVM will exit.");
        // cache-refresher is daemon: JVM exits without waiting for it
    }
}
```

```text
Refreshing exchange rate cache...
Refreshing exchange rate cache...
Refreshing exchange rate cache...
Main thread done. JVM will exit.
```

### How is a daemon thread different from a user thread?

| Feature | Daemon Thread | User (Non-Daemon) Thread |
|---|---|---|
| Prevents JVM exit | No | Yes |
| Default | Inherits from parent | main thread is non-daemon |
| Finally blocks on shutdown | May not execute | Execute normally |
| Use case | Background housekeeping | Critical business logic |
| Created by | `setDaemon(true)` before `start()` | Default (no call needed) |

### What are the rules for daemon threads?

There are four rules to remember:

1. Call `setDaemon(true)` before `start()`. Calling it after throws `IllegalThreadStateException`.
2. Child threads inherit the daemon status of their parent.
3. The main thread is non-daemon.
4. The JVM exits when all non-daemon threads terminate.

```java
// Rule 1: setDaemon after start throws exception
Thread t = new Thread(() -> {});
t.start();
try {
    t.setDaemon(true); // IllegalThreadStateException!
} catch (IllegalThreadStateException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot change daemon status after start");
}

// Rule 2: child inherits parent's daemon status
Thread parent = new Thread(() -> {
    Thread child = new Thread(() -> {});
    System.out.println("Child daemon: " + child.isDaemon()); // same as parent
});
parent.setDaemon(true);
parent.start();
```

### How do you create a daemon thread pool?

Use a custom ThreadFactory that sets daemon status on every worker thread.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DaemonPoolDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4, r -> {
            Thread t = new Thread(r);
            t.setDaemon(true);
            t.setName("daemon-worker-" + t.getId());
            return t;
        });

        pool.submit(() -> System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getName() + " processing"));

        // Pool threads are daemon: JVM exits when main finishes
        // Good for optional background processing
        // Bad for critical work that MUST complete
    }
}
```

### Why are finally blocks not guaranteed in daemon threads?

When the JVM exits because all non-daemon threads are done, daemon threads are killed immediately. The JVM does not run their finally blocks or shutdown hooks attached to those threads.

```java
Thread daemon = new Thread(() -> {
    try {
        Thread.sleep(Long.MAX_VALUE);
    } finally {
        // MAY NOT EXECUTE if JVM exits!
        System.out.println("Saving critical data...");
    }
});
daemon.setDaemon(true);
daemon.start();
// If all user threads end, JVM exits, finally is SKIPPED
```

For critical cleanup, use `Runtime.addShutdownHook()` instead.

### When should you use daemon threads?

Use daemon for background tasks where abrupt termination is acceptable:
- Cache refresh and warming
- Heartbeat monitoring
- Log rotation and periodic flushing
- Metrics collection
- Background cleanup

Use non-daemon for tasks that must complete:
- Trade settlement and payment processing
- Database writes and data migration
- File generation and report creation

### What about the garbage collector?

The garbage collector runs as a daemon thread. It stops automatically when the JVM exits. This is why GC does not prevent JVM shutdown.

### What are common mistakes with daemon threads?

```java
// WRONG: critical work on daemon thread
Thread t = new Thread(() -> settleAllPendingTrades());
t.setDaemon(true); // trades may be lost on JVM exit!
t.start();

// WRONG: relying on finally in daemon for cleanup
Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
    try { process(); }
    finally { flushAuditLog(); } // may not run!
});
t2.setDaemon(true);

// CORRECT: use shutdown hook for critical cleanup
Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
    flushAuditLog(); // runs on SIGTERM, SIGINT
}));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can a daemon thread create a non-daemon thread? Yes, by explicitly calling setDaemon(false) on the child before start().
- What happens to daemon threads during System.exit()? The JVM runs shutdown hooks, then terminates daemon threads.
- Is the main thread a daemon? No, the main thread is always non-daemon.
